Ama Dablam is a mountain in the Himalaya range of eastern Nepal. The main peak is 6,812  metres, the lower western peak is 5,563 metres. Ama Dablam means  &apos;Mother&apos;s neclace&apos;; the long ridges on each side like the arms of a mother (ama) protecting  her child, and the hanging glacier thought of as the dablam, the traditional double-pendant  containing pictures of the gods, worn by Sherpa women. For several days, Ama Dablam dominates  the eastern sky for anyone trekking to Mount Everest basecamp</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:description>
